<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<preface xml:id="pre-purpose">
  <title>Purpose and Audience</title>

  <p>
    This book is the working reference for the PROTEUS Short-Answer project. The
    project studies the mathematical justification visible in short student responses,
    develops a human coding procedure for representing that justification, implements
    an auditable computational workflow, and evaluates how well the resulting human and
    computational records support research.
  </p>

  <p>
    The book serves four connected purposes:
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          introduce the research problem, theoretical motivation, analytic framework,
          and proposed validation study;
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          define the canonical concepts and governing rules used to identify, represent,
          code, and review episodes of justification;
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          train research assistants through a staged sequence of onboarding, blind
          extraction, codebook-assisted coding, calibration, supervised coding, and
          authorized production work; and
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          document the current computational pipeline while preserving the decisions,
          experiments, open questions, and version history needed to interpret the
          project as it develops.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>

  <p>
    The principal audiences are undergraduate research assistants and collaborators 
    evaluating or extending the method. These readers do not need the same material 
    at the same time. The next preface provides reading paths and explains which part 
    of the book governs each type of decision.
  </p>

  <p>
    The project analyzes evidence visible in a response to a particular question. It
    does not treat one short response as a complete account of what a student knew,
    believed, considered, or could do in another setting. Human researchers remain
    responsible for the mathematical reference, episode interpretation, codebook,
    adjudication, and research claims.
  </p>

  <p>
    Computational outputs are therefore proposals and records for human review, not
    automatic research findings. A pipeline may organize responses, propose episode
    structures and code matches, record confidence, or route difficult cases. Those
    outputs must remain linked to the original evidence, governing versions, and any
    later human confirmation, revision, rejection, or unresolved status.
  </p>

</preface>